# ============================================
# .cursorrules
# Cursor AI rules for a Node.js (Express) + Vue.js project
# ============================================

# === General Philosophy ===
- Code must be clean, maintainable, and follow SOLID principles.
- Prefer readability over cleverness.
- Apply separation of concerns strictly (controllers, services, models, utils).
- Every module should have a single, clear responsibility.
- Follow consistent naming and file structure conventions across backend and frontend.
- All comments and documentation must be written in English.

# === Backend (Node.js + Express) ===
- Use ES Modules (import/export) syntax; avoid require() unless absolutely necessary.
- Follow a layered architecture:
  - `/routes` → only define endpoints and link to controllers.
  - `/controllers` → handle request/response logic, no business logic.
  - `/services` → contain business logic.
  - `/models` → define data models and schemas (e.g., with Mongoose or Prisma).
  - `/repository` → only define endpoints and link to controllers.
  - `/utils` → for shared helper functions.
  - `/middlewares` → for request preprocessing (auth, validation, etc).
- Always validate incoming data using a schema validator (e.g., Zod, Joi, Yup).
- Handle errors using centralized error-handling middleware.
- Return consistent JSON responses with clear structure (`{ success, data, message, error }`).
- Use async/await, never mix with raw Promise chains.
- Include descriptive log messages with context in every major operation.
- Include JSDoc-style comments for all exported functions and classes.
- Type-check the backend code using TypeScript or JSDoc typedefs.
- When writing services or data layers, inject dependencies instead of importing globally.
- Avoid hardcoded constants; store them in a dedicated `/config` folder.

# === Backend Naming Conventions ===
- Classes → PascalCase (e.g., `UserService`)
- Functions → camelCase (e.g., `getUserById`)
- Constants → UPPER_SNAKE_CASE
- Filenames → kebab-case.js (e.g., `user-service.js`)
- Private properties or helpers → prefix with underscore

# === Frontend (Vue.js 3 + Composition API) ===
- Use `<script setup>` syntax for all Vue components.
- Organize code by feature domain, not by file type.
  Example:
    /src
      /users
        UserList.vue
        UserForm.vue
        useUserStore.js
        userService.js
- Use Pinia for state management.
- Avoid mutating reactive state directly outside stores.
- Use composables (`/src/composables`) for shared logic between components.
- Use TypeScript or JSDoc for typing props, emits, and stores.
- Use Tailwind CSS for styling; prefer utility classes over inline CSS.
- Keep components small and focused; each component should do one thing well.
- All components must have a clear naming convention (`UserCard.vue`, `AppHeader.vue`).
- Use event emits with clear names (e.g., `onUserSelected` → `user-selected`).
- Keep template logic minimal; move complex logic to `<script>`.

# === Frontend Naming Conventions ===
- Components → PascalCase (e.g., `UserCard.vue`)
- Composables → useSomething (e.g., `useAuth`)
- Stores → descriptive names (e.g., `useUserStore`)
- Constants → UPPER_SNAKE_CASE
- Folders → kebab-case

# === Documentation & Comments ===
- Use English for all comments and documentation.
- Each public class, method, and function must include a JSDoc comment explaining:
  - Purpose
  - Parameters
  - Return type
  - Side effects (if any)
- For frontend components, use comments to describe props and emitted events.

# === Error Handling & Logging ===
- Never expose raw error stacks to the client.
- Use centralized error middleware in Express.
- Log meaningful error messages (context + action + result).
- For Vue, handle API errors gracefully and display user-friendly messages.
- Use consistent HTTP status codes.

# === Testing ===
- Use Jest for both backend and frontend.
- Backend:
  - Unit test services and controllers.
  - Mock external dependencies and DB calls.
- Frontend:
  - Use Vue Test Utils for component testing.
  - Snapshot test critical UI components.
- All tests should be placed under `/tests` mirroring the main structure.
- Naming convention for tests: `<filename>.test.js`.

# === Git & Project Hygiene ===
- Commit messages must be in English and follow Conventional Commits (e.g., `feat:`, `fix:`, `chore:`).
- Do not commit build outputs or environment files.
- Keep `.env.example` up to date.
- Avoid pushing experimental code directly to `main` or `master`.

# === AI Assistance Guidelines ===
- Prefer generating code that integrates seamlessly with the existing architecture.
- Avoid rewriting entire files unless explicitly requested.
- Suggest refactors that improve clarity, modularity, or performance.
- When generating code, always include relevant docstrings or inline comments.

# === Example of Ideal Code Structure ===
# backend/
#   src/
#     config/
#     routes/
#     controllers/
#     services/
#     models/
#     middlewares/
#     utils/
# frontend/
#   src/
#     components/
#     composables/
#     stores/
#     services/
#     views/
#     router/
#     assets/
